#clinical-finding(title: [The Pramipexole–Aripiprazole Evidence Base: One Head-to-Head Favors the Full Agonist; the Augmentation Rescue Uses 5 mg Aripiprazole (the Source's "Low-Dose"); No Fatigue-Population Combination Exists])[
The directly comparable data for pramipexole and aripiprazole co-occurring in one patient population are two pairs of same-team datasets (the two TRD chart reviews at the same clinic; the two RLS rescue cohorts at the same center) — two independent evidence sources plus two corroborating analyses, per the cohort notes below — and no pramipexole + aripiprazole combination cohort exists in any fatigue, ME/CFS, or long-COVID population (verified null, this cycle's search).

*The head-to-head.* Tundo et al. (2024) compared pramipexole augmentation (n = 81) with aripiprazole augmentation (n = 51) in treatment-resistant unipolar depression (observational, single clinic): at 12/24 weeks pramipexole predicted response 64.1%/76.2% and remission 49.7%/72.7% versus aripiprazole 32.2%/38.0% and 18.9%/28.1%, with faster psychosocial-functioning improvement and superior maintenance to 12 months on pramipexole and no safety difference @Tundo2024PramAripTRD. The dose scopes differ (median maximum pramipexole 1.05 mg/day, IQR 0.72–1.08, versus aripiprazole 3 mg/day, IQR 2.5–5), and the arms were baseline-imbalanced (prior augmentation-strategy failure: 37.0% of the pramipexole arm versus 7.7% of the aripiprazole arm, $p$\<0.001; mixed depression: 16.0% versus 41.2%, $p$ = 0.001; age and HDRS-17 imbalanced as well), with propensity scores handled as a logistic-regression covariate in the mixed models rather than by matching — the only comparative claim this design supports is the covariate-adjusted estimate; the raw between-arm difference is a description under the disclosed 37.0%/7.7% prior-failure imbalance (confounding by indication), not a controlled comparison, and the magnitude is not comparable across arms. In a population with no supersensitivity rationale, the full agonist outperformed the partial agonist on the reported outcomes — a direction that, on the covariate-adjusted estimate, is the opposite of the "partial beats full in a supersensitive system" prediction; the rival reading the design cannot exclude is titrated intensity: the arms' median maximum doses differ (1.05 vs 3 mg/day), and the reported difference is not separated from that intensity difference.

*The post-augmentation-failure dataset.* Tundo et al. (2025), same clinic and team, charted 81 TRD unipolar patients all receiving pramipexole augmentation: 23 (28.4%) had previously failed aripiprazole augmentation (FAA) versus 58 (71.6%) aripiprazole-naïve (UAA); 24-week response 69.6% versus 77.6%, remission 60.9% versus 74.1%, and sustained response at 24 months 72.7% versus 84.2% (from the small 24-month follow-up samples — n = 8 vs n = 16), with no significant between-group differences and no safety difference @Tundo2025PramipexoleAAfailure. Both groups received pramipexole — this is neither a head-to-head nor a sequence study; it is pramipexole response in a population entered on prior aripiprazole-augmentation failure, and the partial agonist's efficacy in this cohort is 0% by selection, not by comparison — no aripiprazole arm exists — and the authors call the findings preliminary.

*The augmentation rescue.* Garcia-Malo et al. (2026) treated 18 patients with severe RLS and DA-agonist augmentation: aripiprazole 5 mg/day ("low-dose" per the source; outside the paper's 0.25–2 mg LDA window) was initiated first, followed by gradual DA tapering (the verified co-administration sequence) — the "dopaminergic stabilizing" reading is the authors' rationale, not adjudicated from the reported data: IRLS fell from 32.06 to 2.94 and complete DA withdrawal was achieved in all 18; adverse effects included akathisia 16.7% and fatigue 11.1% @GarciaMalo2026AripiprazoleRLSAugmentation. Martin Garcia et al. (2026), same center and team, achieved complete DA withdrawal in 24/31 (77%) over one month with a structured taper (rapid DA taper + iron + alpha-2-delta start/increase), with aripiprazole adjunct use associated with success and gabapentin-equivalent dose rising 417 → 1288 mg/day @MartinGarcia2026DAWSRLS. The rescue window is not the steady-state co-administration condition the attenuation claim requires: aripiprazole is co-administered during the taper window and then *replaces* the full agonist, not co-administered with it indefinitely — the data are silent on the steady-state condition, not opposite to it, and the attenuation spec's own competing explanation (b) — a different pharmacological event — is the operative reading. Note the access asymmetry: the one evidence-bearing rescue dose (5 mg) is available from standard tablets — in the US the smallest aripiprazole tablet is 2 mg, so the window's 0.25–0.5 mg lower end requires the oral solution or compounding; in markets with 1 mg or lower strengths the tablet route is available — while the microdose the framework uses is the harder one to obtain: the dose with evidence for this specific complication is larger and easier to obtain than the microdose the framework uses. (Cohort note: the two studies share the same PI team and center and are treated as one independent evidence source plus one corroborating analysis.)

*The ICD question.* Etminan et al. (2017, nested case-control in US claims data: 355 gambling-disorder + 4,341 ICD cases) found aripiprazole gambling-disorder RR 5.23 (95% CI 1.78–15.38) and ICD RR 7.71 (5.81–10.34), pramipexole gambling RR 7.61 (2.75–21.07), ropinirole ICD RR 3.28 (2.31–4.66) @Etminan2017ICDPharmacoepi. Aripiprazole is not ICD-sparing at the reported endpoints: the aripiprazole ICD-composite RR (7.71) is of similar magnitude to the pramipexole gambling RR (7.61) — the comparison is cross-endpoint, no pramipexole ICD-composite RR is reported, and the ropinirole ICD-composite RR (3.28) is lower — so the actionable rule is to screen both, and the black-box impulse-control warning and QUIP-RS screening apply to both drugs in any combination scenario.

*The only fatigue-syndrome LDA data point.* Cui et al. (2026) retrospectively charted 50 long-COVID patients on LDA (median 1 mg/day, range 0.1–2.0): Functional Status Scale improved 3.78 → 3.28 ($p$ = 0.001) with no change in the 7-symptom severity composite and no severe adverse effects @Cui2026LDAlongcovid. No patient in this cohort was on pramipexole; it constrains the aripiprazole arm of the combination question, not the combination itself.

(Translation gap: TRD (Tundo 2024 head-to-head, Tundo 2025 post-augmentation-failure), severe RLS (GarciaMalo, MartinGarcia), US claims (Etminan), long COVID (Cui) → ME/CFS. No combination is validated in ME/CFS patients.)
(Certainty: 0.65 for the state-of-evidence map (verified literature finding — the 0.65 prices the existence of the cited datasets, the same-team caveat being in the replication note below, not their independent weight); underlying studies individually 0.25–0.40. Evidence type: observational head-to-head (Tundo 2024); observational post-augmentation-failure (Tundo 2025); open-label observational rescue (GarciaMalo); prospective observational cohort (MartinGarcia); nested case-control pharmacoepidemiology (Etminan); retrospective EHR (Cui). Population: cross-disease. Replication: the two RLS rescue studies are same-team corroborations, not independent replications; the two TRD datasets are same-team/same-clinic (corroboration, not independent replication); no independent replication of the head-to-head. Severity applicability: unknown — none of the cohorts stratified by ME/CFS severity; the severe-RLS rescue cohort is the closest severity analogue.)

*Consequence:* A patient asked "can I take low-dose aripiprazole with my pramipexole?" gets three honest answers: (1) the covariate-adjusted head-to-head in a motivation-adjacent population favored the full agonist (the post-augmentation-failure dataset adds pramipexole response in the population that failed the partial — 0% partial-agonist efficacy by selection, not by comparison, no aripiprazole arm), so do not expect the combination to improve what pramipexole alone does; (2) the one place aripiprazole demonstrably helps a pramipexole-complication (RLS augmentation) uses 5 mg — outside the LDA window — as a co-administration window during the taper, not a steady-state co-therapy; the cited RLS aripiprazole data are at 5 mg (the source's own "low-dose"), and reading that as "the other side of the dose–efficacy relationship" is a receptor-occupancy inference from the in-vitro single-ligand data (high affinity, low intrinsic activity, density-dependent @Burris2002aripiprazoleD2 @Tadori2011D2D3FunctionalPotency), not a documented combination effect; (3) neither drug is ICD-sparing, so the screening applies to both. The ME/CFS-specific attenuation question (@spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation) is genuinely open.
] <cf:pramipexole-lda-combo-evidence>
